european union leaders have endorsed new  plans to tighten defense cooperation  improve military capabilities and inject  new life into the defense industry the  leaders discussed ways to develop use  and share assets together to help Europe  better defend against modern security  threats even as pressure remains high on  military spending the approach back to  the summit in brussels by nato secretary  general anders fogh rasmussen includes  boosting the efficiency and visibility  of EU missions abroad the leaders  announced plans for countries to work  together on remotely piloted air systems  or drones satellite communications  air-to-air refuelling and cyber defense  it's hoped that the projects will create  new jobs and spark more growth in the  industry in international affairs the  council expressed concern about the  crisis in Ukraine where thousands of  people have been protesting since the  government declined to sign a major  partnership agreement with the EU the  leaders underlined that the offer of  signing the association agreement and  trade pact remains on the table and that  Russia should not feel threatened by the  arrangement the association agreement  and the free trade agreement we are  offering is in the long-term interest of  Ukraine to get to have a modern  independent free Ukraine based also on  the rule of law during the meeting  praise was given to France for its  efforts to help avoid a civil war in the  Central African Republic the leaders  expressed deep concern about the crisis  and they've tasked High Representative  Catherine Ashton with presenting a  proposal on ways in which the EU can  help on financial matters the leaders  welcomed recent moves on building a  banking Union which will help to keep  struggling banks of float or wind them  down without major economic consequences  the project including supervision and  resolution mechanisms supported by a  deposit guarantee for citizens faces a  vote in the European Parliament  hopefully before its term ends in May
 

